INT. DELIVERY ROOM - NIGHT

Monitors blink. A woman breathes hard on the bed. A speakerphone sits on the tray beside her, a tinny voice leaking out.

JOANNE KETTERING, 30s, sweat-slicked, grips the bedrail. DR. AMARA NWOSU, 40s, sits at the foot of the bed in scrubs, calm. She lifts one hand to check a monitor, and Joanne squeezes the other.

BERTRAND (V.O.)
(over speakerphone)
I'm on the ferry, I'm on the ferry, I'll be there in twenty minutes.

JOANNE
You said ten.

BERTRAND (V.O.)
There was a delay.

JOANNE
You said ten, Bertrand!

DR. NWOSU
She's strong. Keep going.

JOANNE
I'm going to kill him.

DR. NWOSU
Later. Breathe.

She does. She screams. The monitor spikes. Dr. Nwosu rises, takes her position, and nods to the nurse.

DR. NWOSU (CONT'D)
Now. Push.

BERTRAND (V.O.)
Jo? Jo, is she okay?

JOANNE
She's fine! I'm not!

She pushes. A small cry cuts through the room. Dr. Nwosu lifts a squirming newborn into the light. Joanne collapses against the pillow, laughing and crying at once.

BERTRAND (V.O.)
(over speakerphone, faint)
Is that her? Is that the baby?

JOANNE
(to the phone)
Come meet her, you idiot.
